.\" This page contains Cyrillic: read it with `nroff -Kutf8 -man', not plain
.\" `nroff -man', which renders each letter as two characters of mojibake.
.TH GREP 1
.SH NAME
grep, egrep, fgrep \- search a file for a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
[
.B \-bchlnsvy
] [
.B \-e
]
expression [ file ] ...
.LP
.B egrep
[ option ] ...
[ expression ]
[ file ] ...
.PP
.B fgrep
[
.B \-bchlnsvxy
] [
.B \-e
] [
.B \-f
file ]
[ strings ] [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Commands of the
.I grep
family search the input
.I files
(standard input default)
for lines matching
a pattern.
Normally, each line found
is copied to the standard output;
unless the
.B \-h
flag is used,
the file name is shown if there is more than one input file.
.PP
.I Grep
patterns are limited regular expressions in the style of
.IR ed (1);
it uses a compact nondeterministic algorithm.
.I Egrep
patterns are full regular expressions;
it uses a fast deterministic algorithm that
sometimes needs exponential space.
.I Fgrep
patterns are fixed strings; it is fast and compact.
.PP
Note:
.I egrep
is not on this system.
It is a
.IR yacc (1)
grammar and there is no
.I yacc
here yet;
see
.I cmd/TODO.md
in the source tree.
.PP
The following options are recognized.
.TP
.B \-v
All lines but those matching
are printed.
.TP
.B \-c
Only a count of matching lines is printed.
.TP
.B \-l
The names of files with matching lines are listed (once)
separated by newlines.
.TP
.B \-n
Each line is preceded by
its line number in the file.
.TP
.B \-b
Each line is preceded by the
.B byte offset
in the file at which it begins, counting from zero.
This is useful in locating a match by position rather than by line.
.IP
Note:
v7 printed a
.I block
number here \- the offset divided by a PDP\-11 disk block of 512 bytes.
512 names nothing on this machine, a block here is 3072 bytes, and
.I grep
divided by that constant where
.I fgrep
divided by a hard\-coded 512, so the two commands answered differently
about the same match.
The division is gone rather than the divisor changed:
a byte offset is exact, needs no unit, and both commands now agree.
.TP
.B \-s
No output is produced, only status.
.TP
.B \-h
Do not print filename headers with output lines.
This applies to
.B \-c
as well;
v7's
.B \-c
printed the header regardless.
.TP
.B \-y
Lower case letters in the pattern will also match
upper case letters in the input.
.I Fgrep
implements this too, and matches without regard to case in both directions;
v7's manual page said the flag was
.I grep
only and was wrong about its own program.
.IP
Note:
a byte above 0177 is part of a multi\-byte letter,
which has no case on this system,
so
.B \-y
passes it through unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-e " expression"
Same as a simple
.I expression
argument,
but useful when the
.I expression
begins with a \-.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
The string list
.RI ( fgrep )
is taken from the
.I file.
This is the only way to give
.I fgrep
more than one keyword from a script,
the list being newline\-separated.
.I Grep
has no
.B \-f;
v7's manual page implied otherwise.
.TP
.B \-x
(Exact) only lines matched in their entirety are printed
.RI ( fgrep
only).
.PP
Care should be taken when
using the characters
$ * [ ^ | ? \' " ( ) and \e in the
.I expression
as they are
also meaningful to the Shell.
It is safest to enclose the
entire
.I expression
argument in single quotes \' \'.
.PP
.I Fgrep
searches for lines that contain one of the (newline-separated)
.I strings.
.PP
.I Egrep
accepts extended regular expressions.
In the following description `character' excludes
newline:
.IP
A \e followed by a single character
matches that character.
.IP
The character ^
($) matches the beginning (end) of a line.
.IP
A
.B .
matches any character.
.IP
A single character not otherwise endowed with special
meaning matches that character.
.IP
A string enclosed in brackets [\|]
matches any single character from the string.
Ranges of character codes may be abbreviated
as in `a\-z0\-9'; see EIGHT-BIT TEXT below for what a range means here.
A ]
may occur only as the first character of the string.
A literal \- must be placed where it can't be
mistaken as a range indicator.
.IP
A regular expression followed by * (+, ?) matches a sequence
of 0 or more (1 or more, 0 or 1)
matches of the regular expression.
.IP
Two regular expressions concatenated
match a match of the first followed by a match of
the second.
.IP
Two regular expressions separated by | or newline
match either a match for the first or a match for the
second.
.IP
A regular expression enclosed in parentheses
matches a match for the regular expression.
.LP
The order of precedence of operators
at the same parenthesis level
is [\|] then
*+? then concatenation then | and newline.
.SH "EIGHT-BIT TEXT"
The text on this system is UTF\-8 end to end,
and both commands carry eight bits.
.PP
.I Fgrep
compares bytes and always did,
so a keyword may be any text at all.
.PP
.I Grep
matches
.B bytes,
which is the same rule
.IR ed (1)
follows and which has three visible consequences.
A
.B .
matches one byte and not one letter,
so a two\-byte Cyrillic letter needs two of them.
A
.B *
after a multi\-byte letter repeats that letter's
.I last
byte.
And a range written between two multi\-byte letters,
as in
.RB [ а\-я ],
is a range of the bytes that spell them and not of the letters between them.
.PP
A bracketed class holds each byte value separately \-
the table is 256 bits, one per value \-
so
.RB [ пм ]
matches any of the four bytes that spell those two letters.
.PP
Note:
v7's class table was 128 bits and its matcher masked every input byte with 0177,
which folds the lead byte of a Cyrillic capital onto an ASCII letter:
.RB [ п ]
would have been found inside
.BR ALPHA .
Worse, nothing masked the byte on the way
.I in,
so a class containing a byte above 0177 stored past the end of its own table
and quietly corrupted the compiled expression.
Both are gone.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ed(1),
sed(1),
sh(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if any matches are found,
1 if none, 2 for syntax errors or inaccessible files.
An unknown flag is an error and exits 2;
v7 printed a message and then searched for the bad flag.
.SH BUGS
Ideally there should be only one
.I grep,
but we don't know a single algorithm that spans a wide enough
range of space-time tradeoffs.
.PP
.I Grep
truncates a line at 511 characters
and treats the remainder as a new line,
so a long line may be reported more than once
and the cut may land inside a multi\-byte letter.
.I Fgrep
reads through a ring of 1024 characters:
a matching line longer than that
has had its beginning overwritten by the time the line is printed,
and only its last
.I length
\- 1024 characters come out.
.RI ( v7's
page said 256 for both and was wrong about both.
Both limits are v7's own and neither was changed.)
.PP
.I Grep
matches a starred expression by recursion,
one level per
.B *
that consumes anything,
and says
.B grep: expression too complex
past sixteen levels.
An expression anybody writes goes two or three deep.
Note:
without that limit the recursion runs off this machine's 4096\-word stack \-
and it returns a
.I wrong
answer, an empty one, for a dozen levels before it faults.
.PP
.I Fgrep
holds its keywords in a table of 3000 states \-
one state per character of keyword text, less whatever the keywords share as a
prefix \- and says
.B fgrep: wordlist too large
beyond that.
There is no separate limit on the
.I number
of keywords: five hundred short ones fit easily,
and it takes one keyword of three thousand characters to fill the table on its own.
v7 allowed 6000 states, which does not fit this machine's address space:
a state is four 48\-bit words here and eight bytes on a PDP\-11.
.PP
v7 also carried a second ceiling that no manual page mentioned \-
a 400\-entry queue in the routine that computes the failure links \-
which stopped
.I fgrep
at 399 keywords whatever their length,
gave that same diagnostic when it did,
and overran its own stack frame for a single keyword of about 450 characters.
The queue is sized from the state table now and cannot overflow,
so the 3000 states above is the only limit there is.
.PP
And v7's
.I fgrep
computed some of its failure links wrongly \-
it followed one link from the failing state and then restarted at the root,
where the chain of links has to be followed to its end \-
so a keyword could go unreported although it was present.
Keywords
.BR bd ,
.B debdb
and
.BR ebb ,
against the line
.BR debd ,
is the smallest case: v7 printed nothing.
About one keyword list in a hundred was affected,
and the failure was silent \-
indistinguishable from an honest absence of matches.
Fixed, and checked against 2000 generated keyword lists.
